eai-behavior is BUILD_ONLY (PROTOCOL §2.4): it is never pushed to a registry, and every host that runs BEHAVIOR-1K
tasks builds it locally.

Why: the image is the official stanfordvl/behavior:3.9.2 (BEHAVIOR-1K v3.9.2, the 2026 BEHAVIOR Challenge stack) plus
ffmpeg. It contains NVIDIA Isaac Sim 5.1.0 and Omniverse Kit, whose licences forbid redistribution, and the base has a
13.7 GB layer, above ghcr.io's per-layer limit.

Build (from the repository root; Docker with BuildKit, the default since Docker 23; about 15 GB to pull):
  docker build -t ghcr.io/embodied-agent-interface-v2-internal/eai-behavior:$(cat images/behavior/VERSION) \
    -f images/behavior/Dockerfile .
The tag is the name every BEHAVIOR task uses (environment/Dockerfile, tests/Dockerfile, the standard `sim` service).
The build pulls the public base by digest and installs ffmpeg from the Ubuntu archive snapshot of 2026-09-20; it fails
unless the package state equals dpkg.lock. README.md here shows how to check a build against the tested image.
A host that already built this Dockerfile as rcb-behavior:0.1.1 (the name before protocol v1.0) has the same image and
may tag it instead:
  docker tag ghcr.io/embodied-agent-interface-v2-internal/rcb-behavior:0.1.1 \
    ghcr.io/embodied-agent-interface-v2-internal/eai-behavior:0.1.1

Licences the builder accepts: the NVIDIA Isaac Sim / Omniverse licence terms of the official image (BEHAVIOR-1K's own
code is MIT).

Data, needed to run (never in the image): the licensed BEHAVIOR dataset (the BEHAVIOR Data Bundle, downloaded after
accepting its licence: no redistribution), mounted read-only at run time from $RCB_BEHAVIOR_DATA. The directory holds
omnigibson-robot-assets/, behavior-1k-assets/, 2026-challenge-task-instances/ and omnigibson.key
(docs/benchmarks/behavior.md).

GPU: an RTX-class NVIDIA GPU (Isaac Sim needs one even headless) and the NVIDIA container toolkit.
